# `const pair = [..]; <a branch>; [a, b] = pair;`: the list is written in one block and taken apart in another, and the block that writes it is walked more than once:
# the join once for each literal that a way brings (eachWay, spin), the body of the loop once for each copy of the loop (behindLoop). What each walk made meets in front of the pattern.
# The steps once took the elements by their written names, which are bound to nothing there: the build aborted. Then they read what had met, while the look at the loop went on typing them by the text of the list:
# in spin an integer was previewed and anything came round: the unit was walked again, and under the verifier the build stopped.
# The steps of a pattern are answered out of the text of a list only where list and pattern stand in one block; such a list as these is taken apart as any list is, and look and walk ask one function.
# OPTIONS: check=Validate
# f1 = eachWay, f2 = behindLoop, f3 = spin
